 ## Overview
-R3D Polytope Splitting and Moment Reduction is a voxelization technique that computes exact coordinate moments of the intersection between a 3D polytope and a Cartesian grid by recursively splitting the polytope along grid axes until it fits within a single voxel, then reducing the polytope's moments to that voxel [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Analytic Moment Voxelization](3307-analytic-moment-voxelization.md) by providing the specific geometric splitting and moment accumulation logic required to handle arbitrary convex polyhedra. It differs from [Polynomial Field Integration via Coordinate Moments](3791-polynomial-field-integration-via-coordinate-moments.md) by focusing on the spatial decomposition of the domain (voxels) rather than the integration of a field over a fixed domain.
